    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can u explain the difference between high glycemic index foods and low glycemic index foods? i'm trying to understand how they affect blood sugar for someone managing type 2 diabetes.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The glycemic index (GI) is a measure of how quickly a food raises blood sugar levels after consumption. High GI foods (65+), such as white bread and sugary snacks, are quickly absorbed and cause a rapid spike in blood sugar, whereas low GI foods (55+), such as whole grains and vegetables, are digested more slowly, leading to a gradual and sustained release of glucose into the bloodstream. As a result, managing GI can help regulate blood sugar levels for individuals with type 2 diabetes. Incorporating low GI foods into meals and snacks can aid in maintaining stable blood sugar levels.",
      "source": "target",
